{"id":"696832ef-ea57-4ce9-8749-15fdf9543a0e","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885828,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    sub = {}\\n    def dfs(nid):\\n        res = {nid}\\n        for c in children[nid]:\\n            res.update(dfs(c))\\n        sub[nid] = res\\n        return res\\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        subtree_ids = sub[nid]\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required:\\n            cnt = sum(1 for sid in subtree_ids if node_by_id[sid]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if symbol_counts[sym] - cnt <= 0:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        for sid in subtree_ids:\\n            retained.discard(sid)\\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The task is called \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". From the description and the payload, we have a tree of nodes with IDs, parent references, symbols, depth, removable flag, and utility. There is required_symbols list. The goal: reduce the tree by removing nodes that are removable, while preserving required symbols and the root. The algorithm: consider deeper nodes before shallower, tentative removal only if all required symbols remain represented, use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted? \"sorted retained IDs\" - likely sorted alphabetically or by ID). The output schema requires both arrays of strings.\n\nWe need to parse the case payload which has 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list. The tree structure: nodes with parent reference (string or null for root). Depth is given. We need to build adjacency or parent mapping.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build a dict of node objects keyed by id.\n- Identify root: parent null.\n- Build children list for each node.\n- We need to process nodes in order: deeper depth first, then within same depth sort by utility? Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" Then \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (string compare). But also we need to consider that we may need to compute subtree symbols or check if removing a node affects required symbols. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Means before actually removing, simulate and check if the set of symbols in the tree still covers all required_symbols.\n\nBut careful: Removing a node will also remove its descendants? The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Typically in parse tree reduction, removing a node removes its entire subtree. The node itself has a symbol. The required symbols are a set of symbols that must be present in the tree after reduction. So we need to ensure that after removal, the tree still contains at least one node for each required symbol (including possibly the root?). The root is always retained (never removed). Also nodes with retained children cannot be removed (if a node has children that are retained, you can't remove it? Actually: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), you cannot remove that node. That makes sense because removing a node would also remove its children.\n\nSo the algorithm:\n1. Initially all nodes are retained (except maybe we consider removal). We'll track retained set.\n2. Build a list of all nodes that are candidates for removal: those with removable == True, not root, not required symbol? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove any node that is a root, or whose symbol is in required_symbols, or has any child that is currently retained. So candidates depend on current retained set.\n3. Process nodes in order: deeper first, then id. For each candidate, check if it can be removed: \n   - It must not be root, not required symbol (by symbol), and have no retained children.\n   - Also tentative removal: if we remove it, does the set of symbols in the retained tree (including this node's subtree being removed) still cover all required_symbols? We need to check the symbols of remaining nodes (excluding this node and its descendants). If yes, then we can remove it.\n4. When we remove a node, we add it to removed_node_ids list (in order of removal), and update retained set: remove that node and all its descendants from retained. This may affect the condition for other nodes (since children are now removed, parents might become candidate if they have no retained children).\n5. Continue until no more removable nodes.\n\nWe need to be careful: The order of processing should be fixed based on initial tree? Or dynamic? The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests a fixed order based on depth, not dynamic. Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort all nodes (including those that may later become removable) by depth descending, then id ascending. Then iterate through that list, but only attempt removal if node is currently retained and meets conditions. But if we remove a node, later nodes (shallower) in the list may still be candidates if they satisfy conditions after removal. That's fine.\n\nBut note: The condition \"node with retained children\" means if a node has any child that is still in retained set, you cannot remove it. So after removing descendants, a parent may become removable (if it was not removable before due to retained children). But if we process in order of depth descending, we will process deeper nodes first. So if a deeper node is removed, its parent (shallower) might become candidate when we later process it in the list. That's fine.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not removable initially but become removable later? The fixed list includes them; when we later get to them, they will be processed. That works.\n\nWe need to build descendants map to quickly get subtree nodes for removal and for checking symbol representation.\n\nSimpler: For each candidate removal, we can compute the set of symbols in the current retained tree (or efficiently check if any required symbol only appears in the subtree to be removed). We can maintain a counter of required symbols occurrences in retained tree. Initially count all symbols in all nodes. When tentatively remove a node, we check if any required symbol would have zero count after removing that subtree. If yes, cannot remove.\n\nWe need to maintain counts per symbol. Since nodes may have same symbol multiple times (e.g., s1 appears multiple times). So we need to know if there is at least one node with that symbol in retained set after removal.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Build dict: node_by_id = {node['id']: node}\n- Build children list: children = {id: []} for each id, then for each node, if parent is not None, add child to parent's list.\n- Build subtree_ids: for each node, compute recursively? Could precompute using DFS from root. Since tree size small, we can compute on the fly with a function to get all descendant IDs (including node itself). We'll cache per node? Could store as set in node dict for efficiency.\n\n- Initialize retained set: all node IDs.\n- Initialize symbol_counts: dict from symbol to count of nodes with that symbol in retained set. Compute from all nodes.\n- removed_order = []\n\n- Create list of candidate nodes sorted: all nodes that are removable? Actually we consider all nodes, but we only attempt removal if removable flag is True and symbol not required and not root. We can filter later while iterating.\n\nSorted list: all nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending (as strings). Iterate through that list (once). For each node:\n   - If node['id'] not in retained: skip (already removed).\n   - If node['removable'] is False: skip.\n   - If node['symbol'] in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove required symbol).\n   - If node['id'] is root (parent null): skip.\n   - Check if node has any retained children: for each child in children[node['id']], if child id in retained -> skip (cannot remove because retained children).\n   - Now tentative removal: compute if removal of this node and its descendants would cause any required symbol to have count zero. Compute subtree_ids (including node). For each symbol, if the count of that symbol in retained set minus the count of that symbol in subtree is zero, and that symbol is required, then cannot remove.\n   - If all conditions satisfied: \n        - Add node['id'] to removed_order.\n        - For each descendant id in subtree_ids: remove from retained set; decrement symbol_counts accordingly.\n        - (Note: descendants include node itself.)\n   - Continue loop.\n\nAfter loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained))? Output says \"sorted retained IDs\". Usually alphabetical. We'll sort ascending.\n\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained_node_ids)}.\n\nEdge cases: No removable nodes => removed empty list, retained all sorted.\n\nTest with provided fixtures? We don't have expected results, but we can reason.\n\nWe need to ensure that the root is never removed (it's not removable). Required symbols: root and s1. So nodes with symbol 'root' or 's1' cannot be removed (except root is not removable anyway). \n\nIn fixture, node n2 has removable=true, symbol s2, depth1. n4 depth2 removable true symbol s1? Actually n4 symbol s1, which is required, so cannot remove n4. n6 depth2 removable true symbol s0. n2 has child n4 (required symbol, so n2 has a retained child n4 (since n4 is never removed because symbol s1 is required). So n2 cannot be removed because it has retained child. But if n4 were removable (but it's not), then n2 might become candidate. In these fixtures, n1 (depth1 removable false), n3 depth1 removable false, n5 depth2 removable false. So only nodes that are removable: n2, n4, n6. But n4 is required symbol, n2 has retained child n4, so only possible removal is n6? Check n6: symbol s0, not required, parent n1 (which is not removed), n1 has no other children? n1 has child n6 only. If we remove n6, then n1 has no retained children? But n1 is not removable because removable=false. That's fine. n6 removal: do required symbols remain? root and s1: s1 appears at n1 and n4. n1 remains, n4 remains. So yes. So in first fixture, removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]? But what about n2? n2 has child n4 (s1) which is required and not removed, so n2 cannot be removed. n4 cannot be removed because required symbol. So only n6. That seems plausible.\n\nFor the other fixtures, similar.\n\nBut note: The \"required_symbols\" includes \"root\" and \"s1\". root symbol appears at root. s1 appears at n1 and n4. So removing n6 (s0) is fine.\n\nNow the implementation must be pure Python standard library, no external dependencies. Use json if needed for output? But solve returns a dict, which will be JSON-serialized by the evaluator. So we can just return a Python dict.\n\nWe need to write the solve function inside a Python source string, and then wrap that in a JSON object with key \"implementation\".\n\nBe careful to escape backslashes and quotes in the JSON string.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # ... code ...\\n\"\n}\n\nBut the instruction: \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we need to produce a valid JSON string with the Python code.\n\nWe'll create the implementation as a Python source string, then use json.dumps to produce the JSON string? But we are writing the JSON manually. We'll just write the JSON by hand, escaping characters.\n\nBetter to write the Python code and then escape it. Since we are generating the answer as text, we must ensure proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build structures\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\n        children[nid] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n.get('parent')\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n            break\n    # Precompute subtree descendants (including self) using DFS\n    subtree = {}\n    def dfs(nid):\n        res = {nid}\n        for c in children[nid]:\n            res.update(dfs(c))\n        subtree[nid] = res\n        return res\n    dfs(root_id)\n    # Initial retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Candidate order: all nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed_order = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check tentative removal\n        sub = subtree[nid]\n        # compute if any required symbol would become missing\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required:\n            count_in_sub = sum(1 for sid in sub if node_by_id[sid]['symbol'] == sym)\n            if symbol_counts[sym] - count_in_sub <= 0:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # Proceed to remove\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n        # Remove all in sub\n        for sid in sub:\n            retained.discard(sid)\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n            # Note: if a symbol count goes negative? shouldn't happen\n    # Collect retained IDs sorted\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nBut careful: The subtree computation as above uses recursion that collects all descendants. That's fine but deep recursion could be an issue for large trees? The fixtures are small. It's fine.\n\nWe need to ensure we don't mutate case. We only read.\n\nNow, the output must be exactly JSON with implementation key. We'll produce the code string with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll write the JSON manually. Let's write the code with single quotes to avoid escaping double quotes inside? The Python code uses single quotes for dict keys, which is fine. But we need to escape backslashes and newlines. We'll use triple-quotes? No, we need to embed the code as a JSON string, so we must escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\".\n\nSimplify: Write the code and then apply JSON escaping. Since we are writing the answer, we can manually replace:\n\n- Each newline -> \\n\n- Each backslash -> \\\\\n- Each double quote -> \\\" (but we have none in code if we use single quotes for strings; but we have double quotes in f-strings or comments? None. But we have parentheses and other chars safe.\n\nWe also have single quotes in strings like 'nodes' that need not be escaped. In JSON, single quotes are allowed inside a string? JSON only allows double quotes for string delimiters, so single quotes inside are fine. But we must escape any double quote if present. Our code uses single quotes for string literals, so no issue. However, we have dictionary keys with single quotes, that's fine.\n\nWe also have backticks? No.\n\nSo we just need to represent newlines as \\n and backslashes as \\\\. There are no backslashes in our code except maybe in escape sequences? None. So we can just replace newlines.\n\nWe'll write the code as a single-line string with \\n. But to make it readable, we can write the code with actual newlines, then convert. Since we are generating the final answer, we can do that conversion mentally.\n\nAlternatively, we can use Python's json.dumps on the code string, but we are not running Python. We'll just be careful.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    sub = {}\\n    def dfs(nid):\\n        res = {nid}\\n        for c in children[nid]:\\n            res.update(dfs(c))\\n        sub[nid] = res\\n        return res\\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        subtree_ids = sub[nid]\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required:\\n            cnt = sum(1 for sid in subtree_ids if node_by_id[sid]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if symbol_counts[sym] - cnt <= 0:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        for sid in subtree_ids:\\n            retained.discard(sid)\\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_ids}\"\n}\n\nBut note the variable name conflict: we used 'sub' for dictionary but also 'subtree_ids' later. That's fine. However, inside the dfs function, we refer to 'sub' which is a nonlocal variable; that works. But we also used 'sub' as a local variable name? It's outer variable. Should be fine. Alternatively rename to 'subtree_dict' to avoid confusion. But it's okay.\n\nWe also used 'subtree_ids = sub[nid]' after defining sub. Good.\n\nNow ensure we have a correct newline escaping. We'll write the JSON string with actual newlines and then escape them? No, we need to produce a JSON string where newlines are \\n. So we will write the entire implementation string as a single line with \\n sequences.\n\nIn our answer, we must output exactly one JSON object. We'll do that.\n\nLet's double-check the logic: For required symbol 'root', it's present only at root. Root is never removed. So that symbol is always present. For s1, it appears at n1 and n4 in fixtures. Removing n6 doesn't affect. Good.\n\nOne more nuance: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" The condition we check: any child in retained. After removal, children are removed. So if we remove a child, its parent may become candidate later. That's handled because we iterate through sorted list once, but we might miss a node that becomes removable later but appears earlier in the list? Actually the list is sorted by depth descending, so deeper nodes are processed first. If a deeper node is removed, its parent (shallower) appears later in the list (since depth is smaller). So when we get to the parent later, it will be processed and may be removable. That's correct.\n\nWhat about siblings: removing a node might make its sibling's parent have fewer children? That doesn't affect sibling, but sibling's removal condition (has retained children) is unaffected. So fine.\n\nPotential bug: When we remove a node, we update retained set and symbol_counts. However, the condition \"has retained children\" for other nodes will now correctly reflect children that were removed. Good.\n\nEdge case: A node's removable flag is True, but its symbol is in required_symbols. We skip. But what if a required symbol appears multiple times? We should not remove any node with that symbol even if others exist. The requirement says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So we absolutely cannot remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So our condition is correct.\n\nWhat about the root? It's also a required symbol (since root is in required_symbols), but it's not removable anyway.\n\nNow final output: Return a dict with keys \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". The removed order is a list of strings in removal order. The retained IDs are sorted ascending as strings (default sort). That matches requirement \"sorted retained IDs\". Good.\n\nNow we need to write the answer in the specified format. We'll produce the JSON object with single key \"implementation\". We must not include any other text. Use double quotes for JSON keys and string values. We'll escape as needed.\n\nLet's write the implementation string with proper escapes. We'll produce the code as a single line, but it's fine.\n\nI'll write it step by step:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    sub = {}\\n    def dfs(nid):\\n        res = {nid}\\n        for c in children[nid]:\\n            res.update(dfs(c))\\n        sub[nid] = res\\n        return res\\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        subtree_ids = sub[nid]\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required:\\n            cnt = sum(1 for sid in subtree_ids if node_by_id[sid]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if symbol_counts[sym] - cnt <= 0:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        for sid in subtree_ids:\\n            retained.discard(sid)\\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_ids}\"\n}\n```\n\nBut we need to ensure there are no extra spaces or newlines outside the JSON object. The first non-whitespace character must be { and the last non-whitespace must be }. This is fine.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the implementation string is valid JSON string: all newlines replaced by \\n, and any backslash or double quote escaped. In our code, we have backslashes? No. We have single quotes, no double quotes. But we have newlines. We wrote it as multiline within the JSON string, which is not allowed. So we must replace actual newlines with \\n. In the answer, I will write the implementation string as one line with \\n s